
Model A — record every channel simultaneously
double-click
    -> record VAL for channels 0..7
    -> auto-stop after BPM × multiplier
    -> eight synchronized loops play
Memory
maximum example: 144 KiB
Advantages
perfect phase relationship between all eight recordings;

only one recorder state;

very simple record action;

any channel can later use its captured data.

Disadvantages
records seven channels even when only one is wanted;

no deliberate choice of recording source;

all channels share one start, length, and restart;

does not feel like assigning a musical looper;

changing one recording means replacing all eight.

This is technically simple but not the best instrument workflow.

Model B — one loop mode, one selected channel
assign ADC_LOOP to channel 3
    -> channel 3 is the recording source

double-click
    -> record channel 3
    -> playback through channel 3
Memory
18 KiB maximum
Advantages
minimal memory;

deliberate choice;

mode assignment itself selects the source;

one mode and one recorder;

very easy to understand.

Disadvantages
only one recorded movement at a time;

assigning ADC_LOOP to another channel creates ambiguity:

does it play the existing recording?

does it select a new source?

does it erase the existing recording?

cannot build several independent modulation loops.

This is the least invasive model, but probably too limited musically.

Model C — four loop modes
This is the strongest middle ground.

Add four modes:

ADC_LOOP_0
ADC_LOOP_1
ADC_LOOP_2
ADC_LOOP_3
Each represents one independent loop slot.

channel 2 assigned ADC_LOOP_0
    -> loop 0 records ADC channel 2

channel 5 assigned ADC_LOOP_1
    -> loop 1 records ADC channel 5
The assigned mode makes the deliberate source choice.

Memory
Four maximum-length mono loops:

4 × 18 KiB = 72 KiB
That is half the eight-channel recorder and provides four independently useful loops.

Workflow
1. assign ADC_LOOP_0 to the desired channel
2. choose loop-0 length in the loop layer
3. return to layer 0
4. double-click to arm loop 0
5. recording starts on the next clock boundary
6. recording ends automatically
7. loop 0 plays continuously
Then:

1. assign ADC_LOOP_1 to another channel
2. choose its length
3. record it
4. both loops now play in clock synchronization
This behaves like four modulation-loop tracks.

Which loop does the button operate?
The current system needs a deterministic answer.

Option C.1 — button records every armed loop mode
If channels contain:

channel 2 -> ADC_LOOP_0
channel 5 -> ADC_LOOP_1
a double-click starts both.

That recreates part of the all-channel model and makes independent overdubbing difficult.

Option C.2 — selected loop follows the most recently assigned loop mode
This requires tracking which mode was last edited. That adds hidden state and is not obvious to the performer.

Option C.3 — loop layer determines the active loop
One of the four loop-layer controls is not enough to “select” a loop while also setting all four lengths.

Option C.4 — record the first loop mode that has no completed recording
ADC_LOOP_0 assigned, empty -> record loop 0
ADC_LOOP_1 assigned, empty -> next double-click records loop 1
Simple, but re-recording an existing loop becomes awkward.

Recommended: button A double-click plus held button B loop selection
For example:

A double-click             -> loop 0
hold B + A double-click    -> loop 1?
But this interferes with the existing menu gesture model, so I do not recommend introducing it yet.

Better middle-ground interaction: one active recorder selected in the loop layer
Use one four-parameter loop layer:

LOOP.0 = active loop       0..3
LOOP.1 = loop 0 length
LOOP.2 = loop 1 length
LOOP.3 = loop 2/3 length?
But then one length is missing. That breaks the clean four-loop symmetry.

Therefore, use two loop layers only if independent loop selection is essential:

Loop layer A
├── ACTIVE_LOOP
├── LOOP_0_MULT
├── LOOP_1_MULT
└── LOOP_2_MULT

Loop layer B
├── LOOP_3_MULT
├── reserved
├── reserved
└── reserved
This is structurally ugly and not worth it.

Cleaner proposal: each loop mode is also its recorder selector
The button does not need a separate active-loop selector.

When the double-click occurs, scan the eight channel modes in channel order:

find the first assigned ADC_LOOP_n mode
whose loop state is STOPPED
Record that loop.

Once it contains a recording, the next double-click chooses the next assigned, stopped loop.

Example:

channel 1 -> ADC_LOOP_0, stopped
channel 4 -> ADC_LOOP_1, stopped

first double-click
    -> record loop 0

second double-click after loop 0 is playing
    -> record loop 1
But how do we stop/re-record loop 0 later?

A useful state cycle is:

STOPPED -> ARMED -> RECORD -> PLAY
and the double-click operates on the lowest-numbered assigned loop not currently playing.

When all assigned loops are playing, the next double-click stops all of them. That is simple, but not ideal for individual replacement.

Most performative model: four modes, but record all assigned instances on one take
Reconsidering the workflow, perhaps four independent lengths are less important than four selectable playback identities.

double-click
    -> every assigned loop mode that is currently stopped begins recording
Examples:

One deliberate recording
channel 2 -> ADC_LOOP_0
all other channels -> other modes

double-click
    -> only channel 2 records
Two synchronized recordings
channel 2 -> ADC_LOOP_0
channel 5 -> ADC_LOOP_1

double-click
    -> both record simultaneously
That gives deliberate source choice through mode assignment while still supporting synchronized multichannel recording.

A second double-click:

if any loop is recording
    -> abort recording

otherwise, if any loop is playing
    -> stop all playing loops

otherwise
    -> arm all assigned loop modes
This is deterministic and does not require an active-loop selector.

Dedicated four-value loop-length layer
Use:

LOOP_0_MULT
LOOP_1_MULT
LOOP_2_MULT
LOOP_3_MULT
Each value selects from the existing multiplier table:

64, 32, 16, 8, 4, 2, 1
The table already exists as g_lfoMultiplier. 

So:

loop 0 duration = BPM interval × LOOP_0_MULT
loop 1 duration = BPM interval × LOOP_1_MULT
loop 2 duration = BPM interval × LOOP_2_MULT
loop 3 duration = BPM interval × LOOP_3_MULT
That creates polyrhythmic modulation lengths while retaining the same BPM source:

loop 0 = 1 beat
loop 1 = 2 beats
loop 2 = 4 beats
loop 3 = 8 beats
All start on the same clock boundary but wrap at their individual lengths.

This has strong performative value.

Sync model
There are two possible playback implementations.

Sample-index playback
++playIndex;

if (playIndex >= recordedLength)
    playIndex = 0;
Advantages
minimal;

playback reproduces exactly one recorded sample per runtime iteration.

Problem
The loop is synchronized only by the assumption that runtime FPS remains unchanged.

Small frame-time variations accumulate relative to the BPM clock.

Clock-phase playback
For each loop:

elapsed = g_currentTime - syncStart;
phase   = elapsed % loopDuration;
index   = (phase * recordedLength) / loopDuration;
Advantages
remains synchronized to the system clock;

frame jitter does not accumulate;

all four loops share a timing origin;

playback can survive skipped or slow runtime frames.

Behavior
At each runtime frame, playback asks:

“Given the current clock phase, which recorded sample belongs here?”

rather than:

“What is the next sample after the previous iteration?”

This is much better for a musically synchronized system.

What happens if BPM changes?
Two possibilities:

Fixed-duration playback
The loop keeps the duration measured when it was recorded.

BPM change -> loop keeps old absolute time
This preserves the recorded gesture but loses clock synchronization.

Current-clock playback
The playback duration remains:

current BPM interval × stored loop multiplier
The recorded sample array is mapped over that duration.

BPM change -> loop speeds up or slows down
No interpolation is necessary initially; repeated or skipped sample indices perform the time mapping.

For a musical device, this is preferable:

recorded shape remains
tempo follows system clock
Recording start synchronization
Do not begin immediately at the double-click timestamp.

The double-click should arm recording:

double-click
    -> ARMED
Recording begins at the next active BPM boundary:

next beat
    -> RECORD
That prevents the gesture recognition delay from becoming part of the loop phase.

State:

STOPPED
    |
double-click
    v
ARMED
    |
next beat boundary
    v
RECORD
    |
individual duration reached
    v
PLAY
Because all armed loops begin at the same beat boundary, their recorded and played phases align.

The BPM system already tracks interval and predicted beat/cycle boundaries in g_lfoBpmMatrix. 

The “append playback mode after recording” idea
Model
before recording:
    ADC_LOOP mode unavailable

record a source somehow

after completed recording:
    ADC_LOOP mode appended to roof
Problem
It reverses the intended workflow:

record first
assign playback destination later
Your mode system otherwise means:

choose channel behavior
then operate that behavior
It also introduces dynamic mode-number/roof changes after a take and makes it unclear which channel was the recording source.

The sync problem is secondary. The primary problem is that the performer cannot deliberately declare:

“This channel is about to become loop 0.”

Better solution
Loop modes are always available.

Before a valid take exists:

ADC_LOOP_n mode -> live ADC passthrough
During recording:

ADC_LOOP_n mode -> live ADC passthrough + capture
After recording:

ADC_LOOP_n mode -> synchronized playback
Thus the mode never changes meaning or position in the roof.

Handling duplicate loop assignments
Suppose:

channel 2 -> ADC_LOOP_0
channel 5 -> ADC_LOOP_0
A loop slot can only have one recorded source.

The deterministic rule should be:

lowest-numbered assigned channel owns the recording
all channels assigned that loop mode play the same recording
So:

channel 2 records loop 0
channel 2 plays loop 0
channel 5 also plays loop 0
This makes duplication useful: one gesture can modulate several shader inputs.

No error state is necessary.

Proposed four-loop data
#define ADC_LOOP_COUNT   4
#define ADC_LOOP_SAMPLES 9216
For each loop:

u16             g_adcLoop[ADC_LOOP_COUNT][ADC_LOOP_SAMPLES];

unsigned        g_adcLoopState[ADC_LOOP_COUNT];
unsigned        g_adcLoopSource[ADC_LOOP_COUNT];

unsigned        g_adcLoopLength[ADC_LOOP_COUNT];
unsigned        g_adcLoopRecordIndex[ADC_LOOP_COUNT];

unsigned long   g_adcLoopStart[ADC_LOOP_COUNT];
unsigned long   g_adcLoopDuration[ADC_LOOP_COUNT];
unsigned long   g_adcLoopSyncStart;
Memory:

4 × 9,216 × 2 bytes = 73,728 bytes = 72 KiB
No eight-channel dimension is needed because each loop records only one selected ADC channel.

Capacity versus extreme timing
The extreme example is exactly:

10 BPM
× multiplier 64
× 24 FPS
= 9,216 samples
Therefore:

#define ADC_LOOP_SAMPLES 9216
is sufficient if:

minimum supported BPM is 10;

maximum multiplier is 64;

target runtime rate is 24 FPS.

A round binary capacity:

#define ADC_LOOP_SAMPLES 16384
would use:

4 × 16,384 × 2 = 128 KiB
and provide timing headroom.

But if the frame limiter is reliably 24 FPS and the limits are fixed, 9216 is the exact required maximum.

